name: hello
boss: boot
flavor: The screen is blank and waiting for you.
brief: A terminal is a place where you TYPE to the computer instead of clicking.
brief: You type one word -- a command -- and press Enter. The computer does it
brief: and shows you the answer. That is the whole idea.
brief:
brief: Three commands are all you need to begin. `pwd` says where you are. `ls`
brief: shows what is here. `cd` moves you somewhere else. No flags yet, no
brief: options -- just the bare word and Enter.
brief:
brief: Nothing you type here can break anything. If a command does not work the
brief: screen tells you, and you simply type another. Answers RUN -- type what
brief: you would really type, and if the output is right it counts.
---
q: {print|show} where you are right now -- the directory you are standing in
a: pwd
a: echo $PWD
cmp: ref
ref: pwd
teach: pwd
hint: three letters -- print working directory
---
q: {list|show} what is in this directory
a: ls
cmp: ref
ref: ls
teach: ls
hint: two letters
---
q: {change into @dir@|move into @dir@|put yourself inside @dir@}
a: cd @dir@
a: cd @dir@/
a: cd ./@dir@
why: conceptual
ref: cd
teach: cd
hint: just the command and the folder name -- no flags needed
---
q: {go up one directory|move to the parent directory|go one level up}, with `cd`
a: cd ..
a: cd ../
why: conceptual
ref: cd
teach: cd
hint: two dots means "the directory above this one"
---
q: {go home|return to your home directory} with `cd` and nothing after it
a: cd
why: conceptual
ref: cd
teach: cd
hint: cd with nothing after it always takes you home
---
q: make the shell print the word ``hello`` with `echo`
a: echo hello
out: ^hello$
ref: echo
teach: echo
hint: echo prints back whatever you hand it
---
q: {`clear`|wipe} the screen clean
a: clear
why: conceptual
cmp: ref
ref: clear
teach: clear
hint: one word -- it scrolls the screen clean
